WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.729 --> 00:00:04.310
Everyone's heard of Bird Box — but how deep
have you really dug into it?

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:08.160
We've gone and gathered up some moments in
the movie that you may have missed, so take

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:09.040
off that blindfold.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.300
Here's a look at all the small details you
missed in Bird Box.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:17.000
The first flashback in the film opens on Malorie's
apartment, where her sister Jessica brings

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:18.970
her groceries to restock her fridge.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.420
They also discuss the painting Malorie is
working on.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:25.800
The artwork is filled with people standing
next to each other in the darkness, close

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:26.880
but not interacting.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:30.860
Before we even get to know her, it's a telling
clue to Malorie's mindset.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:34.820
The painting reflects Malorie's fear that
she won't be able to form a loving attachment

00:00:34.829 --> 00:00:39.329
with her unborn child, foreshadowing her later
struggles with the nameless Boy and Girl.

00:00:39.329 --> 00:00:44.329
It also suggests the bigger impending problem
of being isolated from civilization in a world

00:00:44.329 --> 00:00:45.880
ravaged by monsters.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:51.220
"I see a bunch of people sitting together
but they feel incredibly lonely.”

00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:52.760
“The loneliness is just incidental.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.000
It’s really about people’s inability to
connect.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:01:00.400
Right before Gary reveals himself as a servant
of the creatures, he lays out a series of

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:04.740
drawings, showing what seems to be his personal
take on what the monsters look like.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.040
Several of the drawings have distinct features
hinting toward specific supernatural designs,

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:14.420
including one of the most famous cosmic horrors
in the history of literature: Cthulhu.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.560
The cephalopod head of one of the monsters
is subtly threatening and immediately distinguishable.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.340
While the monsters are probably not literally
cohorts of Cthulhu, the drawing seems to be

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.840
a shoutout to one of the founders of modern
horror, HP Lovecraft.

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:32.600
The writer was a master at crafting cosmic
nightmares, often leaning largely on the fear

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.360
of the unknown.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:38.080
Bird Box takes inspiration from a lot of horror
stories that came before it, but the idea

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.260
of a monster so otherworldly that it's impossible
to show on screen owes a major debt to Lovecraft,

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:47.340
likely inspiring this nod to one of the author's
most infamous creations.

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:51.940
When Malorie first reaches the house, society
is in the middle of falling apart.

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:56.810
As the phone and cable networks begin to fail,
one call manages to get through, leading to

00:01:56.810 --> 00:02:01.200
a heart-wrenching scene between two despairing
parents and their child, who clearly sees

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.620
one of the monsters while on the phone.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.700
After rushing out the door without a thought
for their own safety, the couple isn't seen

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:07.710
again in the movie.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.300
Or, at least, they're not seen alive.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:13.560
When the group heads out in a car with blacked-out
windows to gather supplies, they run over

00:02:13.569 --> 00:02:17.989
several speed bumps in the road — one of
which turns out to be the couple's bodies,

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:20.340
lying in the street not far from the house.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:24.450
While their faces are hard to recognize, their
clothes are the same, providing some closure

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.860
for their gruesome fate.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:29.920
"Just a speed bump"

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.939
The scene in Malorie's apartment is actually
a gold mine for little teasers of the apocalypse

00:02:34.940 --> 00:02:35.440
to come.

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:40.000
Another painting, next to the larger piece
she's working on, sports a number of elements

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:42.870
that eerily foretell the events that are about
to unfold.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:47.249
A deer can be seen being hunted down by a
wolf while a ghoulish form hovers over them.

00:02:47.249 --> 00:02:50.969
Add to that the appearance of a bird above
it all, and the whole piece seems to scream

00:02:50.969 --> 00:02:53.180
out with some kind of prophetic foreknowledge.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.640
Malorie can't tell the future, but it's interesting
to note that this specific scenario seems

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:58.640
to be on her mind.

00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:01.240
One way or another, her subconscious saw this
coming.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.120
Sandra Bullock carries Bird Box on her shoulders,
but she's helped by some stellar supporting

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.849
actors, including John Malkovich, who plays
the abrasive Douglas, who wants to survive

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:11.600
at any cost.

00:03:11.989 --> 00:03:16.340
While Douglas' character was made up for the
movie adaptation of the original book, that

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:20.640
didn't make him expendable in fact, he ends
up playing a pretty crucial role in saving

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.520
Malorie and the children's lives.

00:03:22.620 --> 00:03:26.220
Even though he ultimately becomes a hero,
no one really likes Douglas.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:28.580
But you have to give him credit the man is
never wrong.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.600
Everything he says ends up happening.

00:03:30.950 --> 00:03:33.220
He's against his wife leaving the house, and
she dies.

00:03:33.239 --> 00:03:36.430
He resists allowing Olympia in, and she ends
up letting in Gary.

00:03:36.430 --> 00:03:39.260
He doesn't trust Gary, and Gary ends up killing
people.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.640
Even his frivolous lawsuit against his neighbor
who wanted to build a glass house ends up

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.560
being the right call.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:48.080
"It's his property, why do care what he builds?"

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.740
"Because I have to look at it."

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:54.540
Douglas may come off as annoying, but you
have to admit that he's right.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:58.620
Imagine covering up that many windows with
newspapers without getting a glimpse of the

00:03:58.620 --> 00:03:59.320
monsters.

00:03:59.620 --> 00:04:00.340
Fat chance.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:02.940
"I think it's huge mistake."

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:04.180
"Well you would, Douglas.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:05.620
You think everything's a huge mistake."

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:11.320
Much of Bird Box revolves around Malorie and
her kids taking a desperate trip downriver

00:04:11.329 --> 00:04:12.329
in a rowboat.

00:04:12.329 --> 00:04:16.370
The trip takes over two days, and comes with
an incredible number of challenges.

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:20.690
Making the feat even more impressive is the
fact that the character is fairly new to rowing,

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.380
only getting better as she goes along.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:26.880
Bullock does a great job showing this evolution
of skill, as we see her initially flailing,

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.600
whipping the oars around and trying to get
her sea legs as she launches the boat.

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:35.360
By the end, however, she's seen rowing with
focus and determination, like an old pro.

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:39.790
It turns out that this wasn't just acting:
Bullock actually had no experience rowing

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:43.780
when she stepped into the boat, purposefully
leaning on her lack of know-how to make her

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:45.120
performance more realistic.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:49.070
The river scenes were filmed in order, so
her rowing would naturally improve as the

00:04:49.070 --> 00:04:50.100
movie progressed.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:54.140
You can almost see it as a metaphor for parenting
— you kind of just fake it till you make

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:54.640
it.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.680
"We are going on the trip now, it's going
to be rough."

00:04:58.340 --> 00:05:01.440
Malorie's paintings really are the gift that
just keeps on giving.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.340
One extra off-the-wall Easter egg comes in
the form of Catfish host Nev Schulman and

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.480
his wife Laura, who inexplicably appear in
one of Malorie's pieces of art.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:14.810
Shortly after the movie came out, Schulman
share a post on Instagram that highlighted

00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:17.830
the similarities between the artwork and his
real life.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:22.250
He wrote, "Bird Box was scary and all, but
the creepiest part is how much the couple

00:05:22.250 --> 00:05:25.130
in Sandra Bullock's painting looks like me
and Laura."

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:29.220
The resemblance is indeed remarkable, and
it didn't take long to find out why.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.080
Turns out, the resemblance is entirely intentional.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:36.420
In a follow-up post, Schulman admitted he
knew more than he was letting on, writing,

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.430
"Ok ok.

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:41.000
We actually know the artist who did the paintings
for Bird Box and posed for her."

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.860
It's a playful touch from artist Lily Morris,
putting a bit of a fun spin on a more serious

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:46.360
film.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:48.040
Maybe Malorie's just a big Catfish fan.

00:05:48.920 --> 00:05:50.280
"Turn on the news, dumb-dumb."

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:51.100
"Dumb-dumb."

